# 11. There is also a dangerous feedback loop to avoid

Suppose `next_action()` rewards:

```text
number of downstream nodes unlocked.
```

Then agents have an incentive—implicitly—to create graphs where tasks unlock lots of descendants.

Suppose it rewards:

```text
uncertainty reduced.
```

Then the system can manufacture uncertainty.

Suppose it rewards:

```text
claims accepted.
```

Then it optimizes toward easy claims.

This is Goodhart's law territory.

Pāṭala's graph is unusual because **the scheduler can affect the graph it later measures**.

So scoring features must mostly derive from quantities agents cannot cheaply manipulate.

For example:

```text
source dependency structure
existing downstream published use
independent review disagreement
staleness propagation
externally observed user/scholar demand
manually designated corpus priority
```

This is another argument for deterministic policy before learned optimization.

